<TopicPage slug="subjects/welding/foundational/welding-safety-first">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Welding is one of the few trades where the process itself radiates, fumes, spatters and
    shocks — so the discipline begins with safety, not as a formality but as the first
    professional skill. Every welding curriculum (AWS SENSE included) opens here, and every
    incident report explains why: the arc is an industrial sun burning at roughly 6,000 °C in
    open air, the fume it makes is a respirable metal aerosol, the sparks it throws can start
    fires an hour later, and the machine driving it can deliver a serious electrical shock.
    None of these are exotic risks; all of them are present in the first minute of the first
    bead.
  </p>
  <p>
    The professional frame is the <strong>hierarchy of controls</strong>, borrowed from
    industrial hygiene: eliminate or engineer the hazard first (local exhaust ventilation,
    screens, machine design), then administer procedure (permits, fire watches, daily lead
    inspections), and only then protect the person (PPE). Beginners find this backwards — they
    want to start with the helmet and the gloves — but the ordering is what makes welding safe
    as a career rather than safe once: a helmet protects one wearer during one arc, while a
    ventilation system protects everyone in the building for years.
  </p>
  <p>
    This page works through the four hazard families — arc radiation, fume, fire, electricity —
    and the systems of PPE, ventilation and shop practice that answer them. The learning goal
    is not memorising rules but understanding mechanisms, because a welder who knows <em>why</em>
    the shade number matters can read a new situation (a new process, a new alloy, a new site)
    and protect themselves correctly without being told.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four hazards and the systems that tame them" widget="equationStep" config={hazardsCfg} />

  <h2>Arc, fume and fire hazards</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The arc's radiation</strong> is the hazard with no warning system. The arc emits
    intense ultraviolet and infrared alongside visible light; the ultraviolet component causes
    photokeratitis — "arc eye" or "welder's flash" — hours after exposure, with the sensation
    of sand under the eyelids, and it burns unprotected skin like a sunburn in minutes. The
    trap is that there is no warming glow to warn you: the eye does not feel ultraviolet while
    it is absorbing it. This is why helmet shade selection (typically shade 10–13 for stick
    welding at working amperages) and full coverage are enforced from the first lesson — and
    why bystanders matter: at ten metres an arc can still burn retinas and skin, so welding
    screens protect everyone in sight of the arc, not just the welder.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fume</strong> is the slow hazard. Welding fume is not smoke but a respirable
    aerosol of metal oxides and silicates, whose chemistry depends on the electrode, the base
    metal and any coatings. Mild steel fume carries iron and manganese (a neurotoxin at chronic
    exposure); galvanised steel adds zinc oxide, which causes metal fume fever — a flu-like
    illness hours after the work; stainless steel adds hexavalent chromium, a recognised
    carcinogen; painted or coated surfaces add whatever the coating was. Fume particles are
    small enough to reach deep into the lungs, which is why the exposure limits are measured in
    milligrams per cubic metre and why chronic under-protection is the trade's occupational
    disease story.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fire</strong> watches the whole shift. Sparks and slag travel far further than the
    weld — through floor cracks, into rags, along conduits — and smoulder. The standard radius
    for clearing combustibles is about 11 metres (35 feet), plus fire blankets where clearing
    is impossible, plus a fire watch that remains for at least 30 minutes after work stops,
    because smouldering ignitions flare late. <strong>Electricity</strong> completes the family:
    a welding circuit runs tens to hundreds of amps, and shocks happen when the body bridges
    the electrode circuit to the work — wet gloves, damaged leads, coiled cables adding
    resistance and heat, or changing rods with bare skin against the workpiece. The defences
    are procedural and cheap: dry insulated gloves, daily lead inspection, well-clamped work
    leads, and never standing in water while welding.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> fume is just "smoke" and a brief cough is harmless. Welding
    fume is a dose-accumulating exposure, not an annoyance — the industry moved to treat even
    mild-steel fume as a health risk (IARC classifies welding fume as a carcinogen), and the
    ventilation standards exist precisely because the damage is invisible until it is permanent.
  </p>

  <h2>PPE and ventilation</h2>
  <p>
    Welding PPE is engineered to specific hazards, and each item has a reason. The
    <strong>helmet</strong> carries filtered plates graded by shade number — the higher the
    number, the darker the filter; shade 10–13 suits stick and MIG at common amperages, oxyacetylene
    gas welding uses around shade 4–6 because its radiation is gentler. Auto-darkening helmets
    switch in tens of milliseconds, but their fixed-delay sensors mean you still verify shade
    and setting. <strong>Leathers</strong> — jacket or sleeves, gloves (typically gauntlet
    length), spats — block UV and spatter; cotton catches spatter and burns in, synthetics
    melt into skin, so the dress code is flame-resistant fabric with no cuffs for sparks to
    lodge in. Eye protection continues between arcs (clear safety glasses under the hood) and
    after (grinding demands its own faceshield, because grinder injuries outnumber arc injuries
    in many shops' logs).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Ventilation</strong> is the engineering control that outranks every respirator. The
    hierarchy is unambiguous: capture fume at the source with local exhaust ventilation (LEV) —
    a fume-extraction arm or nozzle positioned within about 30 cm of the arc — so the fume
    never enters the breathing zone at all. General dilution ventilation (fans, open doors) is
    the weak second; respiratory protection is the last layer, with filters chosen for the
    contaminant (P100 particulates for fume; supplied air for confined spaces or
    high-exposure work). Welding in a confined space without extraction and gas monitoring is
    the trade's textbook fatal scenario — both for fume and for shielding-gas displacement of
    oxygen, which kills without any smell or struggle.
  </p>

  <h2>Compressed gas cylinders: stored energy in steel</h2>
  <p>
    The gases that shield arcs — argon, carbon dioxide, acetylene, oxygen — arrive in
    cylinders holding gas at pressures up to thousands of psi, which makes cylinder handling
    a safety subject of its own. The rules are physical, not bureaucratic. Cylinders stand
    <strong>upright and chained</strong> (a falling cylinder can shear its own valve and
    become a projectile); valve caps go on whenever cylinders are moved or stored; cylinders
    are moved on proper carts, never rolled or dragged. <strong>Oxygen deserves special
    respect</strong>: it is not flammable itself, but it makes everything else burn faster
    and hotter, so oxygen fittings never touch oil or grease (spontaneous ignition), and
    fuel-gas cylinders are stored apart from oxygen cylinders. Acetylene adds its own
    chemistry — unstable above roughly 15 psi, so its regulators are set low, and cylinders
    used upright (the dissolved solvent can otherwise leak). Regulators are inspected for
    damaged seats and creeping gauges, hoses checked for cracks and protected from sparks,
    and flashback arrestors fitted on fuel-gas lines. The compressed-gas module compresses
    to one sentence: treat every cylinder as a rocket with a valve.
  </p>

  <h2>Shop and site practice</h2>
  <p>
    The permit system (hot work authorisation) exists because the fires that happen are almost
    always the fires nobody watched. A formal hot-work permit — checks completed, combustibles
    cleared, extinguisher staged, fire watch posted, sign-off recorded — turns safety from good
    intentions into an auditable system, and it is legally required in most industrial
    jurisdictions. Around it sits the daily liturgy of good shop practice: leads inspected and
    uncoiled, cylinders chained upright with valve caps on when moved, floors clear of oil
    and offcuts, workpieces grounded through the work lead rather than through building
    structures, and everyone in sight of the arc behind screens.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the fire watch is about putting out fires <em>during</em>
    welding. Its main job is the 30 minutes <em>after</em> — smouldering ignitions in cracks
    and dust layers flare long after the arc stops, and the watch exists to catch exactly
    those.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Shade numbers scale with amperage and process.</strong> The filter's job is to cut
    the arc's radiant output to a safe retinal dose, so requirements rise with current: light
    gas welding at low shades, stick and MIG in the 10–13 band, and heavy-duty or
    high-amperage work pushing toward shade 14. Standards (ANSI Z87.1 and EN 169/379 families)
    publish the tables, and auto-darkening filters are rated by their shade range and switching
    speed — reading those ratings is part of selecting, not just wearing, a helmet.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Exposure limits give fume control its numbers.</strong> Occupational hygienists set
    permissible exposure limits (PELs) and stricter threshold limit values (TLVs) per
    substance, in milligrams per cubic metre over eight hours; manganese, hexavalent chromium
    and nickel carry especially low limits. Ventilation engineering then works backwards from
    those numbers: capture velocity at the arc, duct sizing, filter stage, and — for
    compliance — air monitoring with personal sampling pumps worn by welders on representative
    days. The point for a student is proportion: fume control is a measurable engineering
    problem, not a matter of tolerating a smoky shop.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Electrical safety has its own quantitative layer.</strong> Welders work with open-circuit
    voltages around 60–100 volts DC on many machines — above the thresholds where wet skin can
    conduct dangerous current — and the classic risk factors are well studied: perspiration,
    conductive workpieces, body contact with the electrode circuit. This is why modern machines
    add voltage-reduction devices that drop open-circuit voltage when no arc is struck, why
    regulations distinguish the electrode side (live during arcing) from the work side, and why
    the rule "never change rods with bare skin on the work" survives every generation of
    equipment.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fume control in practice</strong> is a positioning skill as much as an
    installation. The extraction nozzle works by capture velocity — air moving fast enough
    at the arc to pull fume away before it crosses the welder's breathing zone — which is
    why distance is decisive: an arm positioned roughly a hood-diameter from the arc
    captures efficiently; an arm parked half a metre away captures almost nothing while
    running noisily. Welders learn to reposition the arm with the work (magnetic mounts,
    articulated arms), to check airflow direction with a smoke pencil or strip, and to
    recognise the tell-tale of a failing system: fume rising past the face instead of
    sideways into the hood. Body position matters too — never lean your head over the
    joint, keep the plume between hood and sky, and mind cross-drafts from doors and fans
    that can blow the plume back through the breathing zone. In training shops the drill is
    simple: every arc struck with extraction positioned, every day, until the habit is as
    automatic as the helmet — because fume exposure is cumulative, and the welder who
    protects themselves only sometimes is accumulating the dose anyway.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Site work multiplies every hazard.</strong> Confined spaces add gas displacement
    and permit systems; heights add fall protection to hot-work permits; weather adds wind that
    strips shielding gas (and therefore weld quality) and rain that adds shock risk. The
    underlying system stays constant — identify hazard, engineer control, verify, document —
    which is why safety, taught first, is also the skill that transfers furthest across a
    welding career.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> AWS SENSE program guidelines; ANSI Z49.1, <em>Safety in Welding, Cutting and Allied Processes</em>; OSHA 1910.252 hot work provisions.</p>
